lesson k-mixing-colours v=1 format=teach grade=0 unit=4 subject=art levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Mixing colours"
  goal "Say what two of red, yellow and blue make when they are mixed, and mix a green to paint with."

  look {
    scene 30x9 {
      paintpots pots pots=["blue", "yellow"] result="mix" labels=true at=canvas(1, 1)
    }
    say """
      Blue and yellow stirred together make green. Red and yellow make orange, and red and blue make
      purple. Red, yellow and blue cannot be made from other paints, so we start with them.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked k.mix-two k=0 level=medium
    }
    practice k.mix-two count=3 seed=9 easy-count=2 hard-count=2
    practice k.which-two-paints count=2 seed=3
    practice k.mixed-or-not count=2 seed=4 easy-count=1
    show k.paint-a-leaf
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show k.cannot-mix k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show k.lost-paint k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
  }
  remember {
    say "Blue and yellow make green. Red and yellow make orange. Red and blue make purple."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Read each question aloud. Real paint is the best teacher here: a blob of each on a plate and a
    brush to stir them. The leaf is for you to look at; the answer sheet says what to look for.
    Purple is the hardest mix, because a little too much blue makes it dark. If the child is stuck,
    mix the pair in front of them and let them name what comes out. England, Reception, ELG
    Creating with Materials (recalled, not fetched): "Safely use and explore a variety of materials,
    tools and techniques, experimenting with colour, design, texture, form and function".
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level green is mixed in the teacher's pen first, the mixes are green and orange, and Sam has red and yellow."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the mixes are orange and purple, Sam has yellow and blue, and the lost paint is in the purple."
  }
}
